{
  "component": "Input Field",
  "description": "A single-line text entry built as a variant set of ten combinations: five states crossed with two value types. Label, description and error message are each optional through boolean properties.\n\nPURPOSE: Collects short free text such as a name, email address or postcode. The field groups a label, an optional description, the input box itself and an optional error message in one vertical stack.\n\nBEHAVIOUR: The State property covers Default, Hover, Focus, Error and Disabled. Hover and Focus change the border treatment, Focus adds a focus ring, Error switches the border and message text to the danger colours, and Disabled uses the disabled background, border and text tokens. Value Type switches the input text between real content (Default) and placeholder styling (Placeholder), which uses the tertiary text colour. Note that State=Error and Has Error are separate controls, so both need setting for a complete error presentation.\n\nCOMPOSITION: No nested component instances. The structure is plain text layers for Label, Description and Error, plus an Input frame containing the Value text. AI code generators should still check the development codebase for an existing input primitive before building a new one.\n\nUSAGE: Use for short single-line text. Use Textarea Field for multi-line input and Select Field for a fixed list of options. Keep the visible label on; a placeholder is not a substitute for a label. Add the description when the user needs guidance before typing, and the error message only when the field is invalid.\n\nCODE GENERATION NOTES: Map to <InputField> from primitives, or <Input> when Has Label is false. Hover and Focus are browser-driven, so do not encode them as props; expose state through disabled, invalid or similar flags. The label must be tied to the input with a for/id pair, the description and error linked by aria-describedby, and the input marked aria-invalid when in error. The error message needs a live region so it is announced. Placeholder styling comes from the ::placeholder selector, not a separate text layer.",
